  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nWe address the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem using a combination of symmetry enforcement, multi-resolution optimization, and systematic search heuristics. The core idea leverages symmetry (h(x) \u2248 h(2-x) inversion symmetry) which is known to improve by reducing effective dimensionality while a decomposition into \"regions of activity\" helps clarify structure. Our method attempts to optimize candidate function representations across several grid resolutions (starting at 160 points) and uses a set of heuristic-based optimizations that systematically adjust function positions, magnitudes and overlaps. This method allows escaping local minima better than pure stochastic search by incorporating physical intuition about overlap avoidance.\n\nMathematical specification:\n- Function representation: n-point piecewise constant approximation to minimize the overlap integral C5 = max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx with constraints 0\u2264h(x)\u22641 and \u222bh(x)dx=1.\n- Symmetry constraint: h(x) \u2248 h(2-x) (enforcing even function about x=1).\n- Objective decomposition: C5 = max{(1/n) * (-sum(h * h_shift)) for shifts including zero} (equivalent via linearity).\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Validation/sanity: Check n_points must be even due to symmetry constraint, but do not fail for odd.\n2. parent construction: If initial_h_values exists, use it to start optimization.\n3. Symmetry-adapted representation conversion:\n   - If initial_h_values exists and n is even, symmetrize it.\n   - Else generate random initial symmetric profiles (using binary or tapered initializations).\n4. Multi-resolution hill climbing:\n   - Target shifts: start at resolution 160 (parent) or 80, 160, 320, 640, 1280 (if time allows).\n   - For each resolution:\n      * Define grid: dx = 2/n, points from 0 to n-1 (0-indexed).\n      * Enforce symmetry: mirror-index coupling (i <-> n-1-i).\n      * Hybrid optimization:\n        - SLSQP for coarse grid adjustment.\n        - Line search (gradient descent) for local refinement.\n        - Local random jitter for escaping stagnation.\n5. Candidate selection: track best score (minimal C5) across all resolutions.\n\nSearch choices and useful variations:\n- Initializations: use 2-3 different starting conditions per loss improvement to prevent local minima.\n- Resolution scheme: n = [80, 160, 320, 640, 1280] (doubling except 80)\n- Stopping conditions: if no improvement in best score for 2 strategies, reduce time budget for next strategy; if time exceeds budget, return best found.\n\nComputebudget and robustness:\n- Algorithm: SLSQP=1000, LM optimization=500, line search=10.\n- Memory: roughly 3*resolution_datasets simultaneously (retain last top3) per resolution.\n- Time: estimates per resolution: 80\u21925s, 160\u21926s, 320\u21927s, 640\u219215s, 1280\u219230s. Avoid integer overflow by scaling by dx.\n\nValidation and return contract:\n- Validate every candidate: 0\u2264h[i]\u22641, \u222bh dx\u22481 (tolerance 0.1%), and C5 finite: scalar.\n- Return tuple (h_values, bestC5, n_points) with formatted string for solution explanation.\n\nStructure of plan:\n1. Symmetrization and representation conversion.\n2. Hybrid multilevel optimization.\n3. Progressive resolution inclusion with early termination of no improvement path.\n4. Retrials with varied initial conditions on each resolution.",
